That is a powerful addition. Struct-backed or inline tuples give Coral native multi-value returns and lightweight data grouping without needing to invent a separate structural syntax from scratch.

Here is how tuple syntax integrates into the language specifications for functions and variable declarations:

---

## Tuple Types & Syntax

Coral uses a structural tuple syntax built on top of the `struct` keyword, supporting variadic type parameters (`T...`) for multi-value types.

### 1. Function Return Types

Functions returning multiple values use the tuple signature directly in the return position:

```c
struct(i32, bool) get_status() {
    return (200, true);
}

```

### 2. Variable Declarations

Variables holding tuple values are declared using the corresponding tuple type structure:

```c
struct(i32, bool) result;
result = get_status();

```

---

## Complete Examples

### Returning and Unpacking a Tuple

```c
// Function returning a multi-value tuple
struct(i32, cchar*) fetch_user_data() {
    return (42, "Alice");
}

pub i32 main() {
    // Variable declaration using tuple syntax
    struct(i32, cchar*) user;
    user = fetch_user_data();
}

```

This keeps the type system unified—tuples are effectively anonymous structs handled natively by the compiler, avoiding syntax bloat while letting you pass and return multiple values cleanly.